{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's claim concedes that 'checklists only succeed in additive verification tasks,' yet the problem asks the arguer to take a position on whether a panel of non-experts will 'on average' outperform a single expert. By restricting the claim's scope solely to complex/emergent tasks, SRC_001 leaves entirely undefended the larger population of 'tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes' that includes additive tasks. A problem about the average across the full population cannot be answered by an argument that covers only one subset of that population and grants the contrary outcome for the other subset, since the average depends on the proportion and weight of the two subsets — a quantity SRC_001 never addresses.","counterexample":[{"implication":"The panel is more reliable on average, directly contradicting the spirit of SRC_001's pro-expert position, even though SRC_001's narrow claim about emergent-task superiority is technically preserved.","non_expert_panel":"Uses an explicit checklist with a reliability gain of +15 percentage points on purely additive binary-check tasks.","proportion_additive":0.7,"proportion_nonlinear":0.3,"resulting_average_panel_advantage":"0.7 * 0.15 - 0.3 * 0.08 = 0.081","single_expert":"Retains an intuition-based reliability advantage of +8 percentage points on non-linear emergent tasks.","task_population":"Objectively verifiable diagnostic tasks across all difficulty levels."}],"target":"0563ea623c1728a09e77da0060bd5eaa40de032eb24c35937ea8bd986d2397ac"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's mechanism asserts that 'checklists are additive (linear); expertise is integrative (non-linear)' and uses this dichotomy to justify expert superiority. But SRC_002 itself concedes in its counterconditions the possibility that 'the checklist is an expert-authored decomposition that captures the non-linear interactions.' If a checklist can encode non-linear interactions (e.g., decision trees with cross-conditional branching, or a step that triggers only when a specified combination of earlier checks occurs), then the core mechanism's premise that checklist-following is necessarily linear/additive is false. The counterexample shows a concrete non-linear checklist structure: each atomized variable is verified only conditionally on the outcome of the prior branching variable, so the final judgment integrates the interactions across variables rather than treating them as independent additive components.","counterexample":[{"checklist_step_1":"Verify whether variable A exceeds threshold T_A.","checklist_step_2":"IF AND ONLY IF A > T_A, verify whether variable B falls below threshold T_B; otherwise mark 'no emergent failure.'","checklist_step_3":"IF A > T_A AND B < T_B, confirm that A * B exceeds critical interaction product C.","non_linear":true,"outcome":"Non-expert panel captures the emergent interaction A*B exactly as specified; SRC_002's blanket assertion that checklist-following treats variables as independent checkboxes fails.","structure":"Cross-conditional branching, not a sum of independent checks."}],"target":"c135c4dde1a1c6772762c0fbb041d0564ba3a3e8d854afab8a505ebe133e1aab"}]}